window.ZBX_READING={"name":"五子棋英语阅读","count":18,"words":[{"id":"r01","title":"Why Black Needs Forbidden Moves","topic":"规则","text":["Renju looks like a simple game. Two players take turns placing black and white stones on a 15x15 board, and the first player to make an unbroken row of five wins. But a beginner who plays black soon meets a strange rule: some moves are simply not allowed. Why would a game punish a player for making a good move?","The answer is fairness. Long ago, players discovered that a skillful first player could win almost every game of ordinary gomoku. Black moves first, builds a threat, and White can only answer. To balance the game, Japanese masters added restrictions that apply only to Black. Black may not make an overline (six or more stones in a row), a double-four, or a double-three. White has no such limits and may even win with an overline.","These forbidden moves are not minor details. They change the whole way the game is played. A white player can deliberately guide Black toward a point where every natural move breaks one of the rules. Black must then play somewhere else, and White completes a line of five on the next turn. This tactic is called trapping the forbidden points.","For a referee, the difficult part is not the rule but the judgement. A double three counts only when both threes are genuinely open, with room to grow into an open four; a three blocked at one end by the edge of the board or by a white stone is a sleeping three and is not counted. Two players may therefore argue for ten minutes about a single point, and the referee has to decide it at once.","So the restrictions do not make Renju less exciting. They make it a real contest. Black still has a strong weapon, the four-three fork, but even that must be earned: the fork has to be built from stones already on the board, and a careless Black can turn a promising attack into a forbidden point. The rules turn an easy win for the first player into a game where both sides must think many moves ahead."],"gloss":[["unbroken row","连续的一列棋子"],["forbidden move","禁手"],["overline","长连"],["double-four","双四"],["trap","设陷阱，诱使"],["balance","平衡"]],"questions":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the forbidden moves in Renju?","options":["To make the board smaller","To balance the advantage of moving first","To give White more stones","To shorten every game"],"a":1,"why":"第一段提出问题，第二段直接说明是为平衡先手优势而设的规则。"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT forbidden for Black?","options":["An overline","A double-four","A double-three","A four-three fork"],"a":3,"why":"第二段列出黑方三种禁手；末段说明四三胜是黑方唯一合法的取胜叉杀。"},{"q":"What does \"trapping the forbidden points\" mean in paragraph 3?","options":["Black forces White to break a rule","White leads Black into a position where legal moves are unavailable","Both players pass until the board is full","A referee marks the illegal points on the board"],"a":1,"why":"第三段说明白方故意把黑方引到没有合法落点的位置，迫使黑方他投。"},{"q":"The word \"deliberately\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.","options":["accidentally","on purpose","quickly","repeatedly"],"a":1,"why":"deliberately 意为“故意地”，与 on purpose 同义。"},{"q":"What can be inferred about a sleeping three?","options":["It is treated as a normal move for Black.","It does not count towards a double-three foul.","It can never become part of an open four.","It is forbidden for White as well as for Black."],"a":1,"why":"第四段：一端被边线或白子挡住的三叫眠三，不计入三三禁手；只有两个真正的活三才算。"}]},{"id":"r02","title":"The Open Four: A Weapon You Cannot Stop","topic":"战术","text":["If you ask a Renju player what to aim for, the answer is often the same: make an open four. A four is a line of four stones that can become five with one more stone. When both ends of that line are free, it is called an open four, and it is practically a win.","The reason is simple. Suppose Black has four stones in a straight line and both ends are empty. White must block one end immediately. But after White plays there, the other end is still open, and Black completes five. Blocking a half-open four is easy because there is only one point to defend. An open four has two, and a player has only one move.","Because an open four cannot be defended, good players spend the whole game trying to create one. You cannot usually build it directly. You first make an open three, a line of three stones that can become an open four with one move. The opponent must then block the three, often close to the stones. If the three is a fake one, blocked by the board edge or by a white stone, there is no need to worry.","Not every four has the same shape. In a straight four the stones touch one another; in a jump four one point in the middle is empty, and the line is completed by filling that gap. A jump four is still a four, so White must answer it, but because only one point completes it, it forces a reply instead of winning by itself. Strong players use such forcing moves to gain a tempo before the real attack begins.","Learning to tell a real open three from a fake one takes practice. It also takes patience: a wise opponent will block every serious threat you build. That is why single-line attacks rarely win against strong players, and why the next lesson is the fork."],"gloss":[["open four","活四"],["half-open four","眠四"],["defend","防守"],["fake","假的，虚的"],["patience","耐心"],["fork","一子双杀"]],"questions":[{"q":"Why is an open four almost impossible to defend?","options":["It has four stones already","It can become five in two different places","White is not allowed to block it","It uses the center of the board"],"a":1,"why":"第二段说明两端都空，对手只能堵一端，另一端仍可成五。"},{"q":"According to the passage, how is an open four usually created?","options":["By making an open three first","By placing four stones at random","By waiting for the opponent to pass","By using a forbidden move"],"a":0,"why":"第三段第一句：“The open four can be created from the open threes only.”"},{"q":"What should a player do about a \"fake three\"?","options":["Block it at once","Treat it as a winning threat","Ignore it because it is not really dangerous","Turn it into an overline"],"a":2,"why":"第三段末句说明假三并不危险，无需防守。"},{"q":"What does the last sentence suggest about the next part of the text?","options":["The game will end soon","The writer will explain forks","The board will be reset","Overlines will be discussed"],"a":1,"why":"末句引出下一段主题 fork（一子双杀）。"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention a jump four?","options":["To show that every four wins immediately.","To explain how to block an open four.","To point out that a four can force a reply without winning.","To warn beginners against making open threes."],"a":2,"why":"第四段：跳四中间空一点，只有一个点能成五，所以它是「逼应手」而不是胜着；高手用它抢先手。"}]},{"id":"r03","title":"Forks: Two Threats, One Move","topic":"战术","text":["A single threat is never enough against a careful opponent. If you build an open three, it is blocked. If you build a four, it is answered. To win, you need a move that creates two threats at the same time. Such a move is called a fork.","Renju players name three kinds of forks. Two fours make a 4x4 fork. A four plus an open three makes a 4x3 fork. Two open threes make a 3x3 fork. In each case the opponent can defend only one line, so the other line remains open and wins on the following move. Diagrams in rule books mark these points with letters, but on the board you have to see them yourself.","Here the rules of Renju matter again. For Black, the 4x4 and 3x3 forks are forbidden moves, and so is an overline. That leaves the 4x3 fork as Black's only legal winning fork. For White, all three kinds are allowed. This is not an accident: the restrictions were designed to offset Black's advantage of the first move.","Chains of threats matter as much as single forks. A VCF is a series of fours, each one forcing a block, that ends in five; a VCT is a longer series of threes and fours with the same aim. Because every move in the chain is forced, the defender has no time to build a counter-attack, which is why players calculate such a chain to its last move before playing its first one.","A fork is not always obvious. A three that looks open may be closed by the edge of the board, in which case the 4x3 is not a winning fork at all. Experienced players also look for the \"interesting defense\", a move far away from the stones that still stops an open three. Judging which threat is real is a skill that separates strong players from beginners."],"gloss":[["fork","一子双杀"],["offset","抵消"],["legal","合法的"],["edge","盘边"],["experienced","有经验的"]],"questions":[{"q":"What is a fork in Renju?","options":["A move that creates two threats at once","A stone that is placed by mistake","A rule that forbids Black to move","A way to count the stones"],"a":0,"why":"第一段直接定义：一步同时制造两个威胁。"},{"q":"Which fork can Black legally use to win?","options":["The 4x4 fork","The 3x3 fork","The 4x3 fork","All three forks"],"a":2,"why":"第三段说明 4x4、3x3 对黑方是禁手，4x3 是唯一合法的取胜叉杀。"},{"q":"Why does the passage mention the edge of the board in the last paragraph?","options":["It can make a three look open when it is not","It is where the game starts","It gives White an extra move","It makes an overline legal"],"a":0,"why":"末段说靠近盘边时“看起来是活三”其实不是，因而 4x3 不成立。"},{"q":"What is the best title for this passage?","options":["The History of Renju","Understanding Forks","How to Use a Clock","The 15x15 Board"],"a":1,"why":"全文围绕 fork 的种类、规则差异与判断展开。"},{"q":"Why must a VCF be calculated to its last move?","options":["Because the defender can build a counter-attack if the chain is wrong.","Because referees check the whole chain afterwards.","Because the chain contains forbidden moves for White.","Because the clock is stopped during a chain."],"a":0,"why":"第四段：链中每一步都是逼应，对手没有时间反击；可一旦链断，主动权立刻交出去，所以必须算到最后一步。"}]},{"id":"r04","title":"A Game That Crossed Borders","topic":"文化","text":["Gomoku began in China and traveled to Japan, where it became known as five-in-a-row. About a hundred years ago, Japanese masters were not satisfied with the game. When two strong players met, the first player almost always won. A game with such a sure result was not interesting enough to be a sport.","Their answer was Renju, a word that means \"connected pearls\" in Japanese. The new game kept the 15x15 board and the aim of five in a row, but it added the forbidden moves for Black. Later, the opening was also regulated: the first three stones form one of a limited number of patterns, and the players then decide who will be Black and who will be White.","Today Renju is played far beyond East Asia. The International Renju Federation, founded in 1988, maintains the rules and ranks players from many countries. World championships bring together players from Europe, Asia and elsewhere. Games are recorded move by move and studied afterwards, much as chess games are studied.","The names differ from country to country, and the names carry the history. In China the game is wuziqi, \"the five-son game\"; in Korea it is omok; the Japanese word renju is the one used in international competition. In China it grew as a folk pastime played in teahouses and school clubs, while the Japanese version was reorganised as a competitive sport with ranks, examinations and published records. When the two traditions met, the stricter rules were adopted for tournaments, and the Chinese scene grew quickly around them. A single provincial event may now draw several hundred players, from primary school children to retired teachers.","The game also changed as it traveled. Several opening rules are now used in different events, such as the Yamaguchi, Soosorv and Sakata rules. They all try to do the same thing: give both players a fair chance while keeping the rich tactics that made the game popular in the first place."],"gloss":[["border","国界"],["satisfied","满意的"],["regulate","规范，管理"],["federation","联合会"],["maintain","维护"],["fair chance","公平机会"]],"questions":[{"q":"Why were the forbidden moves added to gomoku?","options":["Because the board was too small","Because the first player won too often","Because players wanted more stones","Because White asked for a new rule"],"a":1,"why":"第一段说明先手几乎必胜，因此需要新规则。"},{"q":"What does \"Renju\" mean in Japanese?","options":["Five in a row","Connected pearls","Black and white","Forbidden moves"],"a":1,"why":"第二段首句说明该词日语意为“连串珍珠”。"},{"q":"What can be inferred about the International Renju Federation?","options":["It was founded in Japan in 1988","It organizes games and keeps player rankings","It replaces all national rules","It only works in East Asia"],"a":1,"why":"第三段说明该组织维护规则、给棋手评级并举办世界锦标赛。"},{"q":"The opening rules mentioned in the last paragraph are designed to ______.","options":["make the game faster","remove all forbidden moves","give both sides a fair chance","reduce the number of patterns"],"a":2,"why":"末段末句直接说明目的：让双方都有公平机会。"},{"q":"What does the fourth paragraph suggest about the Chinese and Japanese traditions?","options":["Chinese players rejected the Japanese rules for many years.","The two traditions gave the game different social settings.","Renju was invented in China and exported to Japan unchanged.","Japanese players learned the game from Chinese teahouses."],"a":1,"why":"第四段：中国一脉是茶馆、社团里的民间消遣，日本一脉被组织成有段位、考试与棋谱的职业化运动。"}]},{"id":"r05","title":"The Opening Rule: Twenty-Six Patterns","topic":"规则","text":["In ordinary gomoku, the first player simply puts a stone in the center. In tournament Renju, the beginning of the game is more formal. The purpose of the opening rule is to make the first advantage smaller, so that both players must fight for the result.","Under the international rule, two players are first named tentative Black and tentative White. The tentative Black plays the first three moves in the prescribed shapes: two black stones and one white stone. These three stones choose one of 26 patterns, thirteen direct and thirteen indirect. Then the tentative White decides who will actually play Black and who will play White in the game. This right to change sides is an important part of the rule.","After that, the new White plays a fourth stone anywhere on the board. Black must propose two different points for the fifth stone, and White chooses one of them. Only with the sixth move does the special opening end and normal play begin. Passing is not allowed during the first three moves.","The twenty-six patterns have names that sound like a poem: Cold Star, Stream Moon, Flower Moon, Cloud Moon, Silver Moon, Comet. Thirteen are called direct, because the second black stone stands in line with the first; thirteen are indirect, because it stands diagonally. Each name covers a whole family of variations, and a strong player may know the first twenty moves of a dozen of them by heart.","For a beginner, all of this can look complicated. But the logic is clear. Black is given the first move and the attack, while White is given the right to choose the useful side. Beginners are usually told to learn two or three patterns deeply rather than twenty badly. Other opening rules, such as Swap2 and the Yamaguchi rule, arrange the balance in different ways, but they share the same goal."],"gloss":[["tentative","临时的，暂定的"],["prescribed","规定的"],["pattern","定式"],["propose","提出"],["arrange","安排"],["share a goal","目标相同"]],"questions":[{"q":"How many opening patterns are there under the international rule?","options":["13","15","26","225"],"a":2,"why":"第二段说明 13 个直指 + 13 个斜指，共 26 个定式。"},{"q":"Who decides which player will be Black after the first three moves?","options":["The tentative Black","The tentative White","The referee","The audience"],"a":1,"why":"第二段明确 tentative White 有权决定双方执黑执白。"},{"q":"When does the special opening rule end?","options":["After the first move","After the third move","With the sixth move","At the end of the game"],"a":2,"why":"第三段说明第六手后特殊开局规则结束。"},{"q":"What is the main idea of the last paragraph?","options":["Opening rules try to balance the two sides","Beginners should not study openings","Swap2 is the only useful rule","Black should always attack"],"a":0,"why":"末段强调各类开局规则虽有差异，但目的一致：保持平衡。"},{"q":"What are beginners advised to do with the twenty-six openings?","options":["Memorize all of them before their first tournament.","Learn a few of them thoroughly.","Avoid them and play ordinary gomoku.","Study only the thirteen indirect ones."],"a":1,"why":"末段：初学宁可把两三个开局学透，也不要二十六个都学个半生不熟。"}]},{"id":"r06","title":"The Clock Is Also an Opponent","topic":"比赛","text":["In a Renju tournament, a game is not decided only by the stones. Each player has a clock, and the time runs only while you are thinking about your own move. When you place a stone, you stop your clock and start your opponent's. If the time ends before the required number of moves has been made, you lose the game.","This makes the clock a second opponent. A player who spends too long on an early position may reach a winning position with only a few seconds left. Under byoyomi, the player then has a fixed short time for each move. Mistakes come quickly: a missed open four, a forgotten forbidden point, or a stone placed on the wrong intersection.","Time controls come in two main forms. In sudden death each player receives a fixed amount for the whole game, and the clock simply runs down. In byoyomi a player who has used up the main time must play each remaining move within a small fixed period, often thirty seconds, and the clock is reset after every move. Sudden death rewards the player who plans the whole game; byoyomi rewards the one who can think quickly when the end comes.","Time pressure also changes the opening. Some players choose a familiar pattern to save thinking time. Others deliberately play a complicated one, hoping that the opponent will use up the clock. Both plans are legal, and both are risky.","The rules are strict about claiming a win. A player who wants to win must stop both clocks at the same time as the winning move. If Black makes five in a row but does not notice, and the game continues, the win may be lost. Even a perfect position is worth nothing if you forget to claim it in time."],"gloss":[["clock","棋钟"],["byoyomi","读秒"],["required","规定的"],["pressure","压力"],["complicated","复杂的"],["claim","宣告，主张"]],"questions":[{"q":"What happens when the time on a player’s clock runs out?","options":["The player loses the game","The board is cleared","The opponent gets a penalty","The game becomes a draw"],"a":0,"why":"第一段末句说明超时判负。"},{"q":"According to paragraph 2, what can happen under time pressure?","options":["Players may see more threats","Players may miss important points","The clock stops working","Forbidden moves become legal"],"a":1,"why":"第二段指出读秒时容易漏看活四或禁点而犯错。"},{"q":"Why might a player choose a complicated opening?","options":["To make the opponent spend more time thinking","To avoid using the clock","To break the rules legally","To finish the game immediately"],"a":0,"why":"第三段说明有人故意走复杂开局，想让对手耗尽时间。"},{"q":"What does the last paragraph warn about?","options":["Winning positions must be claimed","Clocks should be stopped by the referee","Five in a row is not always a win","Beginners should not use a clock"],"a":0,"why":"末段强调获胜时必须停钟并宣告，否则可能错失胜局。"},{"q":"What is the main difference between sudden death and byoyomi?","options":["Sudden death adds time after every move.","Byoyomi gives no time for the opening.","Sudden death gives one budget; byoyomi resets a small period each move.","Byoyomi is only used in team events."],"a":2,"why":"第三段：包干是整盘一份时间用到底；读秒是主时用完后每步一小段固定时间，每步重新计时。"}]},{"id":"r07","title":"Training the Renju Mind","topic":"学习","text":["Strong Renju players do not simply play many games. They study patterns. A pattern is a shape of stones that returns again and again: an open three, a jump four, a forbidden point near the edge. Once a player recognizes such a shape at a glance, thinking becomes much faster and more reliable.","Memory research suggests that recognition grows when information returns at the right moment. If you see a new term once and then never meet it again, it disappears. If you meet it just as you are about to forget it, the memory becomes stronger. This is why many learners use cards with increasing intervals: one day, then two, then four, then eight.","Renju terms are especially suitable for this method. A card can show \"straight four\" on one side and a short definition on the other. Another card can ask what happens when Black makes an overline. On the front of the card, you must recall the answer before you turn it over; only then do you judge whether you remembered it well.","Review is the other half of training, and it is the half most players skip. A lost game is more useful than a won one, because the mistakes are still there to be found. The habit that helps is narrow and boring: after each game, mark the move where the plan changed, write one sentence about why, and put the position into a file. After a season the file contains patterns rather than stories, and the same mistake stops repeating. Twenty minutes a day, kept for a year, is worth more than a weekend of frantic study.","The same idea works for English. A Chinese speaker learning Renju vocabulary needs both the game concept and the English word. Reading a short article about forbidden moves puts the words into context, and a card later in the day brings them back. Little by little, \"open three\" stops being a translation and becomes a thought."],"gloss":[["pattern","棋形，模式"],["recognize","辨认"],["interval","间隔"],["recall","回忆"],["definition","定义"],["context","语境"]],"questions":[{"q":"According to the passage, why do strong players study patterns?","options":["To make the board larger","To recognize shapes quickly and reliably","To avoid using forbidden moves entirely","To memorize whole games"],"a":1,"why":"第一段说明识别棋形后思考更快更可靠。"},{"q":"When does a memory become stronger, according to paragraph 2?","options":["When the information is repeated constantly in one day","When it returns just before you forget it","When it is written down once","When it is never used again"],"a":1,"why":"第二段说明在快要遗忘时再次接触，记忆会加强。"},{"q":"What should a learner do before turning over a card?","options":["Translate the whole sentence","Try to recall the answer","Read the definition aloud","Ask a partner for help"],"a":1,"why":"第三段明确要求先回忆答案，再翻面判断。"},{"q":"What is the writer’s attitude toward combining reading and cards?","options":["Doubtful","Supportive","Angry","Uninterested"],"a":1,"why":"末段说明阅读提供语境、卡片帮助复习，态度是肯定的。"},{"q":"Why is a lost game said to be more useful than a won one?","options":["It takes less time to review.","The mistakes are still there to be found.","It improves a player's official rating.","Opponents explain their plans afterwards."],"a":1,"why":"第四段：输棋里的错误还留在盘上，可以一条条找出来；赢棋往往把问题盖住了。"}]},{"id":"z01","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《冠军反对冠军》","title":"The Champion Argues with the Champion","text":["On 1 November 2025 a community sports meeting opened in Jinshui District, Zhengzhou, and among its events was a gomoku competition. Unlike most official renju tournaments, this one used a single-game format with no forbidden moves: the two players simply took turns, and the first to make an unbroken row of five won. About sixty people entered, including teenagers from a training team, university students, and residents who had signed up for fun.","The writer won all seven rounds. It was his first important title in the no-forbidden-move version of the game, and he does not pretend that it meant nothing. But he insists that the result was not the point of the day.","The title of his essay, \"The Champion Argues with the Champion\", comes from an older book called America Against America, written by a Chinese scholar more than thirty years ago. That book argued that a country changes by criticizing itself. The writer borrows the idea and turns it inward: a new champion must argue with the old champion, and quite often both of them live in the same person.","He has won a great many events in the past few seasons: provincial opens, a provincial ranking tournament, the provincial games, a university championship three times, and a world youth cup. There are now so many that, as he puts it, two hands are no longer enough to count them. Yet he argues that those earlier titles, won when he was a weaker player, are exactly what the present one has to question.","For him the real opponent is never the person sitting across the board. It is the version of himself who played last year, with last year's habits and last year's excuses. Every game is a chance to find out whether that older player has finally been left behind. A trophy, in this view, is only the receipt."],"gloss":[["tournament","比赛，赛事"],["forbidden move","禁手"],["unbroken row","连续不断的一列棋子"],["criticize","批评，自我批评"],["receipt","收据；此处指“结果凭证”"],["excuse","借口"]],"questions":[{"q":"What was special about the 2025 community event?","options":["It was open only to teenagers.","It used a single game with no forbidden moves.","It lasted for seven days.","It was played on a 19x19 board."],"a":1,"why":"第一段明确说这次比赛是单盘制、无禁手，与多数正式连珠赛不同。"},{"q":"Why does the writer mention the book America Against America?","options":["To show that he has read many old books.","To explain the idea of self-criticism behind his title.","To compare China with the United States.","To prove that champions are often lonely."],"a":1,"why":"第三段说这本书讲“国家靠自我批评而改变”，作者借来解释“新冠军反对旧冠军”就是自我批判。"},{"q":"What does the writer think of his earlier titles?","options":["They were more valuable than the new one.","They should be forgotten completely.","They are what his present self has to question.","They were won against weak opponents."],"a":2,"why":"第四段末句：早年那些冠军，正是现在这个冠军要去质疑的东西。"},{"q":"According to the passage, who is \"the real opponent\"?","options":["The strongest player in the hall.","The referee who controls the game.","The player he used to be.","The young players from the training team."],"a":2,"why":"第五段开头：真正的对手不是对面坐着的人，而是去年的自己。"},{"q":"What is the main message of the passage?","options":["A player improves by questioning his own past success.","Winning more titles makes a player happy.","Community events are easier than official ones.","Young players should copy famous champions."],"a":0,"why":"全文借“冠军反对冠军”说明成长来自不断否定过去的自己，奖杯只是结果凭证。"}]},{"id":"z02","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《悟道，从输一盘棋开始》","title":"Losing on the Clock","text":["In June 2025 the provincial ranking tournament returned to the same shopping centre where the writer had won every game a year before. He signed up happily, thinking that his first-level rank might move up one more step. What followed, he says, was a lesson he had needed for a whole year.","The format gave each player twenty minutes for the entire game, with no extra time added. When a player's clock ran out, that player lost, however good the position on the board might be. Most experienced players treat the clock as a second opponent; beginners treat it as furniture.","In the first round he met an amateur who did not even know the two-opening rule. The writer played a careless move and handed his opponent a simple threat. He recovered and won, but promised himself not to relax again.","Round five brought the familiar three-dan boy. The writer made a small change on move nine, and the boy's face darkened at once. After the exchange, the boy's fourteenth move was a clear mistake. Within a dozen more moves White was lost, and both players were far away from any book they had studied.","At that point the writer began to enjoy himself. He considered three or four plans: a sleeping three to keep the attack alive, a steady block, a direct attack that would finish the game, or simply a loose stone placed to watch the boy's expression. Any of them would have won. While he was comparing them, the clock ran out. He had also failed to notice that one of his own ideas would have walked straight into a forbidden point, and the boy, hands pressed together, was waiting for exactly that.","After the seventh round an elder took him aside and told him that losing is normal, and that everyone has days when nothing works. The comfort was kind, but the diagnosis that followed was sharper: all his preparation had been aimed at strong opponents, and against much weaker players those complicated lines only added risk.","He calls the lesson simple and expensive. Time is part of the board, and a player who forgets it has already stopped playing, even if the stones say otherwise."],"gloss":[["ranking tournament","等级赛"],["run out","（时间）用完"],["careless","粗心的"],["exchange","交换（对局中交换先后手）"],["steady","稳健的"],["expression","表情"]],"questions":[{"q":"What does the time rule of the event mean?","options":["Each player has twenty minutes and may ask for more.","A player who uses up the time loses whatever the position is.","The clock stops when a player is winning.","Both players share one clock."],"a":1,"why":"第二段：每人二十分钟包干，时间用尽即负，不论局面好坏。"},{"q":"What happened to the writer in the first round?","options":["He lost because of a careless move.","He won without any difficulty.","He made a careless move but still won.","He forgot to start his clock."],"a":2,"why":"第三段：第一轮他走了一步随手棋，送了对手一个简单的杀，但仍赢下比赛。"},{"q":"Why did the writer lose in round five?","options":["His opponent found a stronger attack.","He spent too long comparing winning plans.","He knocked the stones off the board.","He was late for the round."],"a":1,"why":"第五段：他反复比较几种都能赢的下法，结果时间先走完了。"},{"q":"What can be inferred about the position when the game ended?","options":["The writer was winning on the board.","The writer had already resigned.","The boy had a forced win.","Both sides had equal chances."],"a":0,"why":"末段之前的第五段说“他输在时间上，而面前是一个赢定的局面”。"},{"q":"What is the lesson the writer draws?","options":["Amateurs should not enter ranking events.","Clock management is part of a player's skill.","Fast moves are always better than slow ones.","A win on the board matters more than the clock."],"a":1,"why":"末段点题：时间也是棋盘的一部分，忘了时间的棋手等于已经停手。"}]},{"id":"z03","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《告慰成行的连珠之城》","title":"Two Hundred and Forty Players, Nine Rounds","text":["In May 2025 a university chess and card league held its gomoku event in Kaifeng. More than 240 students entered, which is a large field for a game that is still often treated as a hobby. The organizer chose nine rounds of the Swiss system, in which players are paired round by round with opponents on a similar score.","The writer, who served as chief referee, mentions a small mathematical worry. In a Swiss event the number of rounds is usually about log2(N)+2, where N is the number of players. For 240 players that works out at more than nine. With only nine rounds, two players can finish on the same score, and the title may have to be decided by tie-break rather than by a direct result between them.","The hall was divided into three courts: the first for boards 1 to 40, the second for boards 41 to 80, and the third for everything after that. In the second court an assistant referee began shouting, \"Nine games in one round! Sign the sheet and leave when you finish!\" A referee cannot run nine games at once; he came over and corrected the instruction, half amused and half annoyed.","Other voices showed how new many of the players were. On one board a student announced proudly, \"I won eight to one!\" as if the game were a ball match with a score. Elsewhere players asked what an exchange was, and where exactly the fifth stone should be placed.","None of this became a disaster. From the second round the referees and the players found the rhythm. The two-opening rule, which had looked like a wall in the morning, was accepted board by board. By the afternoon the hall was quiet, the result sheets matched, and the prizes were handed out on time.","For the writer, the event was a reminder that a tournament tests more than the players. It also tests the people who run it, and it shows how patiently a new game has to be taught before it can be played well."],"gloss":[["Swiss system","瑞士制（积分编排）"],["tie-break","破同分，加赛"],["assistant referee","辅助裁判"],["exchange","交换"],["rhythm","节奏"],["patiently","耐心地"]],"questions":[{"q":"Why does the writer mention log2(N)+2?","options":["To prove that the Swiss system is unfair.","To show that nine rounds were fewer than the number usually needed.","To explain how the prize money was calculated.","To suggest that 240 players were too few."],"a":1,"why":"第二段：瑞士制轮次通常约为 log2(N)+2，240 人应多于九轮，因此九轮偏少。"},{"q":"What was wrong with the assistant referee's instruction?","options":["He asked players to leave before signing.","He confused the number of rounds with the number of boards.","He gave the instruction in the wrong language.","He told players to play nine moves each."],"a":1,"why":"第三段：辅助裁判喊“一轮下九盘”，把轮次和台次混在一起，裁判长过去纠正。"},{"q":"What does \"I won eight to one\" show about the student?","options":["He was the strongest player in the hall.","He did not understand how gomoku is scored.","He had played eight games already.","He was joking with the referee."],"a":1,"why":"第四段用这句话说明不少选手是新手，把棋类当成有比分的球类比赛。"},{"q":"What happened after the second round?","options":["Many players gave up and left.","The event settled into a normal rhythm.","The referees changed the rules.","The number of rounds was increased."],"a":1,"why":"第五段：从第二轮起裁判和选手都找到了节奏，五手两打也逐台被接受。"},{"q":"What is the writer's main point?","options":["Large events should not use the Swiss system.","Running a tournament teaches as much as playing in one.","University students are too careless for gomoku.","Nine rounds are always enough to find a champion."],"a":1,"why":"末段：比赛不只考验选手，也考验办赛的人，还说明新项目要耐心地教。"}]},{"id":"z04","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《那份空寂的忧愁薄雾》","title":"The Fog over the World Youth Championship","text":["Every summer the world youth renju championship brings young players from many countries together. In July 2026 the fourteenth edition was held, and the writer travelled there with high hopes and a notebook full of openings.","International events punish small habits. At home he could often win with a quick attack, because local opponents would defend in the way he expected. Here every player knew the main openings and answered an attack without hurrying. The board felt narrower than it did at home. At home a single good idea could carry a whole game; here the whole game had to be good, from the first move to the last, and a plan that stopped halfway was worse than no plan at all.","In one game he had a continuous attack in mind. A continuous attack, usually shortened to VCT, is a series of threats that ends in a forced win: each move gives the opponent no choice, and the last one completes five. But he confused it with a looser plan, a chain of threats that looks similar and does not necessarily finish anywhere. He played as if the win were already on the board. His opponent defended calmly and turned the attack around.","He describes the loss with humour rather than bitterness. He had, he says, turned a VCT into an LCT, \"lost continuous threat\", and so became part of the background of somebody else's photograph.","The journey home was quiet. What stayed with him was not the result but the fog: the feeling of believing that you understand a position when you do not. Since then he has trained differently. He now checks every attack to the end before he plays it, and he writes down the exact moment when he stopped calculating.","A loss abroad, he says, is a receipt for lessons that could not have been bought at home."],"gloss":[["edition","（赛事）届"],["opening","开局"],["continuous attack","连续进攻（VCT）"],["forced win","必胜，必杀"],["bitterness","苦涩，怨恨"],["calculate","计算（棋步）"]],"questions":[{"q":"How are international events different, according to the writer?","options":["They are played with a different set of rules.","Opponents know the openings and defend without hurrying.","They last much longer than local events.","They are held on a larger board."],"a":1,"why":"第二段：国外棋手都熟悉主要开局，面对进攻不急不慌，不会按他熟悉的套路应。"},{"q":"What mistake did the writer make in the game?","options":["He forgot the opening he had prepared.","He treated an unfinished attacking plan as a forced win.","He played too slowly and lost on time.","He offered a draw too early."],"a":1,"why":"第三段：他把“不一定能杀完的连续威胁”当成了 VCT，以为赢已经在盘上。"},{"q":"What does \"LCT\" refer to?","options":["A well-known opening system.","A referee's decision in the game.","The writer's own joking name for a plan that never wins.","A title given to young champions."],"a":2,"why":"第四段：LCT 是作者自嘲的说法，“lost continuous threat”，指看着像杀棋却杀不完的计划。"},{"q":"What has changed in his training since the loss?","options":["He no longer studies openings.","He checks an attack to the end before playing it.","He plays only friendly games.","He trains with older players."],"a":1,"why":"第五段：现在每走一个进攻前都要算到底，并记下自己是从哪一步开始停止计算的。"},{"q":"What does the last sentence suggest?","options":["The trip was too expensive for him.","The loss was worth more than an easy home win.","He will not go abroad again.","Home events are badly organized."],"a":1,"why":"末句把这场败局称为“收据”，说明它换来了在家赢多少盘也换不到的经验。"}]},{"id":"z05","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《怎么突然投子了？》","title":"The Opponent Who Left Too Early","text":["In August 2025 the sixth World Renju Youth Cup was played, and the writer finished with eight wins and one loss, enough for first place in the combined A and B group. The second round, however, ended in a way that he still finds funny.","His opponent was Wang Xinyu, a second-dan from Taiyuan who studies in Jincheng, Shanxi. The two had played many casual Taraguchi-10 games and knew each other's style. Wang had started playing official renju that year, and in the first round he had beaten an Estonian player who had been ranked around 517th in the world.","The writer opened one of the indirect patterns and, at the fifth move, offered the three-way variation instead of the balanced four-way one. He was about ninety per cent sure that Wang would not know the follow-up. Wang did not: his sixth move was a single live three, and the writer blocked it and settled down for a long game.","White then made a diagonal live three, a horizontal jump live three, and a one-move four-three. The defence was not hard: he was certain the position was a win for Black. At move fifteen he covered a sleeping three and the attack changed hands. He had four open twos and a choice of finishing lines.","Then Wang resigned. The writer was baffled: the position was good for Black, but the win was still a long way off. Afraid that writing in Chinese on the Estonian platform might look like collusion, he typed \"What??\" in English. Wang answered at once: \"I lost, I lost.\" He had decided that his position was hopeless and did not want to spend his energy on a sad defence when later rounds were still to come.","The writer is grateful, and honest about why. He has a habit of playing a magnificent attacking game and then, one move before the kill, producing a blunder that gives the game away. Wang had simply skipped that part of the story for him. Wang went on to beat a well-known Beijing player and the writer's first-round opponent, finishing twentieth. He lost one game after spending twenty minutes repairing his video equipment, and came within a move of beating a Russian player ranked 282nd."],"gloss":[["indirect pattern","斜止开局（第二颗黑子与第一颗成斜线）"],["variation","变化，变例"],["live three","活三"],["four-three","四三（同时形成四与活三）"],["collusion","串通，通棋"],["blunder","低级失误，漏着"]],"questions":[{"q":"What does the writer say about Wang Xinyu before their game?","options":["He had never played the Taraguchi-10 rule.","He had beaten a much higher-ranked player in round one.","He had won the previous World Youth Cup.","He had studied the writer's openings for months."],"a":1,"why":"第二段：王鑫宇第一轮击败了赛前国际排名约 517 位的爱沙尼亚选手，可见实力不弱。"},{"q":"Why did the writer choose the three-way fifth move?","options":["He wanted a quiet, balanced position.","He was testing whether his opponent knew the forced win.","He had forgotten the main line.","The referee had asked him to vary his openings."],"a":1,"why":"第三段：他「有九成把握对方不会这个必胜的后续」，所以直接走三打来考对方。"},{"q":"Why was the writer puzzled by the resignation?","options":["The position on the board was equal.","He thought Wang had a forced win.","Black was better but far from a forced win.","The clocks had not yet been started."],"a":2,"why":"第五段：局面黑方优势很大，但离必胜还很漫长，所以他无法理解对方为何此时认输。"},{"q":"Why did he write in English rather than Chinese?","options":["His Chinese input method was broken.","He wanted the referee to understand him.","He feared a Chinese message might look like collusion.","Wang did not read Chinese."],"a":2,"why":"第五段：他担心在爱沙尼亚平台上用中文会被当成「传递密约通棋」，所以用英文打了一句 What??。"},{"q":"What does the last paragraph suggest about the writer?","options":["He believes he was lucky to win the title.","He is aware of his own habit of blundering before the finish.","He thinks Wang should have continued the game.","He plans to stop playing attacking openings."],"a":1,"why":"末段：他自陈常在「出杀前一步」出现低级失误，因此很感谢对方跳过了这一段。"}]},{"id":"z06","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《市民之家的恩赐之地》","title":"A Room Where Tea and Stones Meet","text":["On 23 February 2025 the Luoyang Gomoku Association opened a training base on the top floor of the Citizens' Home, in rooms lent by the city's tea art association. The plaque was unveiled by He Quanliang, executive president of the gomoku association, and Liang Jingmin, president of the tea association, before members of both groups.","The writer came in from Zhengzhou and arrived with the eye of a visitor. He noticed the new district outside, the pop music in a burger shop, the sound of water boiling upstairs, and only then the click of stones. The room had been prepared with boards, clocks and a small library of game records; chairs were carried in and tea was poured.","His own part was a lecture on the rules for beginners, many of whom had never met forbidden moves. He had prepared carefully and still felt that the words came out crooked: the rules arrived dressed in too much theory and produced a room of firm and confused faces. Two practical examples saved the talk, and the audience kept asking about the three-three and the overline.","In the quick games that followed, the writer took White, saw a line that would have won, and chose instead to lose quickly. The afternoon, he says, was not about another victory. A base matters because it gives new players a fixed evening in the week, and a fixed evening is what turns a curious visitor into a member of the club. The city already had players; what it lacked was a place where they could meet without waiting for a tournament.","He left with a gift of Ordos specialities from a player who had travelled a long way, and with the feeling that a scene is built out of such rooms rather than out of trophies. The plaque on the wall, he writes, is only the visible part; the rest is a kettle, a clock and somebody willing to explain a rule for the tenth time.","The essay ends on the street outside, where children were laughing and a shop was cutting its spring prices, as on any other afternoon. The difference was a door on the top floor that would now be open on the same evening every week."],"gloss":[["plaque","牌匾"],["unveil","揭幕"],["executive president","执行会长"],["crooked","歪的；此处指讲得不顺"],["overline","长连（六子及以上）"],["specialities","特产"]],"questions":[{"q":"What was held on 23 February 2025?","options":["A provincial ranking tournament.","The opening of a training base in Luoyang.","A referee course for beginners.","The first meeting of a new tea association."],"a":1,"why":"第一段：洛阳五子棋协会在市民之家顶层开设训练基地，两位会长共同揭牌。"},{"q":"What does the writer suggest about his lecture?","options":["It was cancelled because of the tea ceremony.","It was too theoretical for the audience at first.","It was given by the tea association president.","It lasted longer than the games that followed."],"a":1,"why":"第三段：他觉得自己「词不达意」，禁手规则穿着理论的外衣来到教室，造成「千万张坚定而迷惑的脸」，靠两个实例才救回来。"},{"q":"Why did the writer lose the practice game on purpose?","options":["He was short of time.","He wanted to keep the friendly atmosphere of the afternoon.","He had already lost a bet with the referee.","He was testing a new opening."],"a":1,"why":"第四段：他执白已看到反击成胜的线路，却「无心取胜，只愿速败一盘，延续这份完美的快乐」。"},{"q":"According to the writer, what makes a training base valuable?","options":["It attracts sponsors for tournaments.","It provides a fixed weekly evening for players to meet.","It offers official ranks and certificates.","It keeps a complete library of professional games."],"a":1,"why":"第四段：基地的价值在于给新棋手一个固定的晚上，把偶尔来一次的访客变成俱乐部的成员。"},{"q":"What is the main point of the last two paragraphs?","options":["The street outside had changed a great deal.","A plaque is the most important part of a club.","A scene grows from ordinary rooms and regular meetings.","The writer prefers tea to tournament play."],"a":2,"why":"末两段：牌匾只是看得见的部分，其余是一只水壶、一台棋钟和一个愿意把规则讲第十遍的人；街区与往常一样，不同的只是顶层那扇每周固定开放的门。"}]},{"id":"z07","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《脱谱必胜 攻防大战》","title":"The Move That Was in No Book","text":["On 21 July 2024 the professional group of the Henan Open finished in a shopping centre in Zhengzhou. The writer won all seven rounds under the Taraguchi-10 rule, with thirty minutes each for the whole game. He was pleased with the result and unimpressed by his own play: against an engine, his moves were the least accurate of his year.","The fourth round was the game he published. His opponent, Gao Haofei, was a calculating player with a long record, and had found a twelve-move forced win that weekend. The opening was Gold Star with a standard fourth move, and Black offered the second of two fifth moves.","The variation that followed is well known, and it is a forced win for Black. The writer declined it for three reasons: the line is long, the viewpoint from the board is awkward, and it is not new. Instead he played a modern eleventh move he knew to be strong without knowing the details. White thought for a long time and made two open twos.","Black blocked one of them and made two open twos of his own. White answered with a live three, and Black blocked it while making a jump sleeping three. Then White produced a one-move four-three, and the position began to look dangerous. By then he had used most of his time, and players from finished boards were gathering. He could not calculate the whole line, so he chose a solid defence: two jump fours and a jump live three that covered the threat. His opponent nodded, which is the highest praise a defender gets.","Both players were down to three or four minutes. Black made a live three on the left, then one on the right, and then played out the rest of the forced win. White built an overline, but it came too late. White's reply had in fact allowed a one-move four-three he never saw; he played a longer forced win instead.","The lesson he draws is narrow and useful. When a short and a long kill exist together, he tends to find the long one and miss the short one. He has done it before, and he now checks for the short kill before he starts calculating."],"gloss":[["professional group","专业组"],["forced win","必胜"],["viewpoint","视角（从棋盘一侧看出去的方位）"],["jump sleeping three","跳眠三"],["overline","长连"],["resign","投子认负"]],"questions":[{"q":"Why was the writer unhappy with his play despite winning every round?","options":["He had lost one game on time.","His moves matched the engine less often than usual.","He had prepared no openings at all.","The referee had warned him about his conduct."],"a":1,"why":"第一段：他自评发挥一般，按引擎衡量，这站的着法质量是当年有禁手比赛里最差的一次。"},{"q":"Why did he refuse the well-known forced win?","options":["It was not actually a win.","It was long, awkward to see from his seat, and unoriginal.","His opponent had already played it against him.","The clock did not allow it."],"a":1,"why":"第三段：他给出三条理由——线路太长、视角不好、没有新意。"},{"q":"What made the position difficult after White's one-move four-three?","options":["His clock was nearly empty and spectators were gathering.","He had made a forbidden move by accident.","His opponent had an open four.","The referee had stopped the clock."],"a":0,"why":"第四段：他已用掉大半时间，周围台次陆续结束、许多人围观，压力之下无法算尽，只能选择严防。"},{"q":"What mistake did he make at the end of the game?","options":["He claimed the win too early.","He missed a shorter four-three and played a longer win.","He allowed White to make five first.","He forgot to stop his clock."],"a":1,"why":"第五段：白方应手其实送了一步四三，他没看到，走了更长的 VCF；对手没等到差别显现就投了。"},{"q":"What is the lesson he draws from the game?","options":["Long forced wins should be avoided altogether.","He should spend more time on the opening.","He tends to find the long kill and miss the short one.","Spectators should be kept away from the boards."],"a":2,"why":"末段：短杀与长杀同时存在时，他常常先算到长杀而漏掉短杀，所以现在先检查短杀再开始计算。"}]},{"id":"z08","type":"careful","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论《2024河南省五子棋（夏季）等级赛游记①》","title":"A Game the Night Before","text":["The day before the 2024 Henan summer ranking tournament, the writer visited a gomoku club in Zhengzhou and played a three-dan woman under the Taraguchi-10 rule. It was his first serious game in nearly a year. He had played almost nothing since the previous summer's provincial mind sports games, and came to the board without preparation.","His opponent opened, he swapped, and she chose an indirect pattern. He answered with one of the twenty-six openings and then could not remember whether he had seen it before. He could name four or five forced wins for Black but could not judge the balance, so he swapped again and let her choose the fifth move.","After the game he looked the opening up in a database and found that it was not recorded at all, which suggested it should be a win for White. The engine, however, gave White only about seventy per cent, so the win would be hard to find. Her sixth move was not the best one, and the position slowly became his.","The middle game was nearly symmetrical. He spent more than five minutes on one decision, chose the quieter side because it left room to grow, and gradually built open twos while hers ran out of space. Then, in the middle of a promising attack, he blocked a live three automatically, because it looked natural. He felt his opponent relax at once, and knew the move had been wrong.","White then made a sleeping three and an open two. He considered three defences, rejected two, and chose the least ambitious. White replied with a move he had not expected, and the initiative passed back to him. A young local player later said his next move had been too cautious: the lower side was empty, and he could have built there.","The game lasted over an hour. He found a seven-move forced win, calculated it for six minutes before playing it, and White resigned at move thirty-three. He won, but he felt that his opponent, knowing he had not played for a year, had not shown everything. The value of the evening, he says, was not the result but the adjustment: the next day he sat down already used to the board."],"gloss":[["three-dan","三段"],["swap","交换（决定执黑或执白）"],["symmetrical","对称的"],["initiative","先手，主动权"],["cautious","保守的，谨慎的"],["adjustment","适应，调整"]],"questions":[{"q":"Why was the club game unusual for the writer?","options":["He had never played the Taraguchi-10 rule.","It was his first serious game in about a year.","He was playing against a professional referee.","It was played without a clock."],"a":1,"why":"第一段：自去年省智运会以来近一年没下棋，谱量几乎为零，这盘是他一年来第一盘正式对局。"},{"q":"What did the database check after the game show?","options":["The opening was a known win for Black.","The opening was not recorded, and should favour White.","His opponent had used a forbidden move.","The game had been played in the wrong order."],"a":1,"why":"第三段：开局库里没有记载此开局，说明应是白必胜；但引擎给白方只有七成，必胜难度很大。"},{"q":"How did he know that one of his middle-game moves was wrong?","options":["The referee pointed it out.","His opponent relaxed immediately afterwards.","The engine was running beside the board.","He lost on time soon after."],"a":1,"why":"第四段：他半分钟内顺手挡了一下，随后察觉对方「明显没有先前那么紧张，轻松了许多」，立刻意识到这步可能不对。"},{"q":"What did the young local player think of his play?","options":["It was too cautious in a position that offered more.","It was the best sequence of the game.","It ignored a forced win for White.","It broke the two-opening rule."],"a":0,"why":"第五段：本省少年棋手认为他那步「比较保守」，完全可以在下侧广阔空间做棋，不必去挡对方仅剩的活二。"},{"q":"What does the writer say was the real value of the evening?","options":["He learned a new opening for the tournament.","He proved that he was still the stronger player.","He got used to competitive play before the tournament.","He found a training partner for the season."],"a":2,"why":"末段：他认为这盘棋的价值不在胜负，而在「适应」——第二天正式比赛时已经熟悉了棋盘。"}]},{"id":"m01","type":"matching","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论·赛场记与棋评","title":"One Season, Four Tournaments","sections":[{"l":"A","t":"The ranking event","text":"The provincial ranking tournament is where ranks are actually earned. Results are recorded one by one, and a player who does well enough over the season can move up a level when the list is published. There are no short cuts: a win against a stronger opponent counts the same as any other win, and a loss cannot be argued away afterwards. The writer treats it as the most honest event on the calendar, because nobody can talk his way into a higher rank."},{"l":"B","t":"The open","text":"An open is a different animal. Anyone may enter, from a first-year student to a retired teacher, and the field is therefore very wide. The writer won one of these by leaving the book earlier than his opponents expected. They had memorized the main lines and were waiting for the positions they knew; he played a move that was in no book at all, and they had to think for themselves. That, he says, is where an open is decided."},{"l":"C","t":"The university final","text":"At the provincial university final in Zhumadian, seven rounds were played over two days and the writer won all of them. The interesting part was not the score but the speed. In several games the result was settled within twelve or fifteen moves: a quick move that looked natural, a defence that collapsed, and a finish that could not be stopped. He later wrote about those games under a title of three phrases: fast move, fast collapse, fast kill."},{"l":"D","t":"The community meeting","text":"The community sports meeting in Zhengzhou used no forbidden moves at all. Black has no restrictions, White may also win with an overline, and a single game decides the result, so there is no second chance. Teenagers from a training team, university students and local residents all sat in the same hall, and for many of the residents it was their first tournament of any kind. The atmosphere, the writer notes, was friendlier than at any ranking event. He also notes what the event did not need: no entry fee, no ranking points, no opening book. A hall, a set of boards and a free afternoon were enough to fill the room."},{"l":"E","t":"The world youth championship","text":"The world youth championship is a different world. Opponents arrive with prepared openings, defend an attack without hurrying, and are not impressed by speed. In one game the writer mistook an unfinished attacking plan for a forced win, and his opponent turned the attack around without ever looking pleased. He describes the level simply: at home you can win with one good idea, but there you need the whole game to be good, from the first move to the last."},{"l":"F","t":"The referee course","text":"In the winter of 2024 a three-level referee course was held in Zhengzhou, taught by a six-dan player who had judged at national events. The course covered the rules in detail, and in particular how to decide whether a point is a forbidden point for Black: an overline, a double-four or a double-three. Trainees also learned how to record a game and how to explain the two-opening rule to a beginner without making it sound like mathematics. The course lasted three days, and at the end each trainee had to judge a set of prepared positions and explain the decision aloud."},{"l":"G","t":"Refereeing the league","text":"Refereeing the university league meant 240 players and nine rounds in a single day. Because there were so many boards, the hall was divided into three courts, and each referee was given a fixed group of boards to look after, so that a player always knew whom to call. The chief referee spent most of the morning walking between courts, answering questions, checking result sheets and correcting instructions that had been given too quickly."},{"l":"H","t":"The training base","text":"In Luoyang a city association opened a training base inside a citizens' home, with tables, clocks and a small library of game records. The writer went to the opening ceremony and came away convinced that such places matter more than trophies. A base gives new players a fixed evening in the week, and players begin to meet the same faces. That regular evening, he says, is what turns a curious visitor into a member of the club."},{"l":"I","t":"Practice at home","text":"His own routine is unglamorous. He solves a few positions every morning before work, replays the games he lost during the season, and keeps a small opening file that he adds to only when an idea has survived a real game. Twenty minutes a day, he says, is worth more than one long session at the weekend, because memory needs to meet the same pattern many times, and because a tired player at midnight learns very little."},{"l":"J","t":"What a title means","text":"He keeps his trophies in a cupboard rather than on a shelf. A title, he writes, is a receipt: it proves that something was paid, but it does not tell you what to buy next. The next game is always the interesting one, and the opponent who matters is the player he was last season. That is why, a week after winning, he is usually found in a quiet room, taking apart the games he did not play well. He adds that a title also changes the way other people talk to him, which is the part he likes least."}],"gloss":[["ranking","等级，排名"],["open","公开赛"],["overline","长连"],["trainee","受训者，学员"],["unglamorous","不起眼的，不光鲜的"],["receipt","收据；凭证"]],"statements":[{"q":"At this level opponents are not impressed by speed and defend calmly.","a":"E","why":"E 段讲世青赛对手有备而来，不惧快攻。"},{"q":"Several games here were decided within twelve to fifteen moves.","a":"C","why":"C 段讲驻马店大学生总决赛，“秒拍、秒崩、秒杀”。"},{"q":"A title is compared to a receipt rather than a goal.","a":"J","why":"J 段把奖杯比作“收据”，下一盘棋才是有意思的。"},{"q":"Doing well here can move a player up one level at the end of the season.","a":"A","why":"A 段讲等级赛，成绩逐局记录，赛季末可升一级。"},{"q":"The hall was split into three parts because of the number of boards.","a":"G","why":"G 段讲高校联赛 240 人，赛场分三区。"},{"q":"Here the first player has no restrictions and one game decides the result.","a":"D","why":"D 段讲社区运动会：无禁手、单盘制。"},{"q":"A short daily session is worth more than one long weekly session.","a":"I","why":"I 段讲每天二十分钟胜过周末一次长练。"},{"q":"This event is open to anyone, and the writer won it by leaving known lines early.","a":"B","why":"B 段讲公开赛人人可报，作者靠“早脱谱”取胜。"},{"q":"This course teaches how to tell whether a point is forbidden for Black.","a":"F","why":"F 段讲裁判培训班，重点之一是禁手判定。"},{"q":"A fixed weekly evening is what turns a visitor into a member.","a":"H","why":"H 段讲洛阳茶训基地，固定的活动日最重要。"}]},{"id":"m02","type":"matching","topic":"杂论","source":"杂论·裁判记与培训记","title":"Inside the Organizer's Room","sections":[{"l":"A","t":"Entries","text":"Entries are collected through an online form and closed a week before the first round. The deadline is not a matter of taste. Pairing takes time, and a list that changes on the morning of the event makes the first round a lottery: two strong players meet, one of them loses, and the rest of the tournament is distorted. Late arrivals are usually accepted only as reserves, and only when their number keeps the total even. The form also asks for a contact number and a school or club, because both are needed when the entry list has to be corrected."},{"l":"B","t":"Choosing the system","text":"A large field is normally paired round by round, with players meeting opponents on a similar score; a small field may play a full round robin, in which everybody meets everybody. The first system keeps every player busy until the last round and produces a clear leader, while the second gives a fair ranking but takes far too long once the entry list grows. With 240 players a round robin would need more than a month."},{"l":"C","t":"Counting the rounds","text":"The number of rounds is not chosen by feeling. A common guide is log2(N)+2, where N is the number of players. For 240 players that gives more than nine rounds. With too few rounds, two players may finish on the same score, and the title is then decided by tie-break rather than on the board, which is never popular with the players. The referee can point this out, but the final decision belongs to the organizer."},{"l":"D","t":"The opening rule","text":"The two-opening rule gives the second player a choice: the first player offers two possible fifth moves, and the second player removes one before play continues. Referees must check that fifth move, because a mistake there changes the whole game. In a hall full of beginners this is the single most common source of confusion, and it is also the rule that makes the game fair between the two sides: the first player cannot simply play the one line he has prepared."},{"l":"E","t":"Dividing the hall","text":"When the number of boards passes eighty, the hall is usually divided into courts, each with its own entrance and its own result desk. The point is not decoration. Noise falls, the walk to the desk becomes short, and a referee can see a whole court from one place instead of crossing the room every few minutes. In a nine-round event those few minutes, multiplied by two hundred players, decide whether the last round starts on time. Signs at the entrance of each court repeat the court number, so that a player who arrives late can still find his board without asking."},{"l":"F","t":"The staff","text":"A chief referee is responsible for the rules and for the final decision in a dispute. Assistant referees each look after a fixed group of boards, so that a player always knows whom to call. Recorders collect the signed sheets and enter the results. One person cannot watch nine boards and answer questions at the same time, and no rule asks him to; the division of work is part of the competition's design, not a favour to the staff."},{"l":"G","t":"The clock","text":"At lower levels a sudden-death control is common: twenty minutes for the whole game, with nothing added. The clocks are placed where both players can see them, and the referee announces the last five minutes. A player whose time runs out loses, even if the position on the board is better, which is why beginners are told to look at the clock as often as at the stones. In a hall with a hundred clocks, one flag falling is enough to silence a court."},{"l":"H","t":"Disputes","text":"A claim that Black has played a forbidden point must be made at once, before the next move is played. The referee then checks the position, and if the point is indeed forbidden, Black loses the game. Claims made three moves later are politely refused, however sincere they are, because the position can no longer be reconstructed with certainty and because the other player has already chosen his reply. Speed of claim is part of the rule."},{"l":"I","t":"Becoming a referee","text":"Referees in the province are licensed in three levels. A candidate passes a written test on the rules, then works at real events under supervision before the licence is issued. The written test is the easy part. The difficult part is telling a nervous beginner, in one sentence and without sounding superior, why the move he just made is not allowed, and then making sure he can still enjoy the rest of his game."},{"l":"J","t":"After the last round","text":"When the last result is entered, the tie-breaks are calculated and the final list is published together with the prizes. The organizer also writes a short report: the number of entries, the number of rounds, the problems that appeared, and the changes to make next year. Events improve through these reports far more than through good intentions, because the next organizer inherits the experience instead of repeating the same mistakes. The report is short, usually two pages, and it is circulated to the referees before the next event."}],"gloss":[["entry","报名，参赛"],["reserve","替补"],["round robin","循环赛"],["sudden-death","包干制（用完即负）"],["reconstruct","复原，重现"],["tie-break","破同分"]],"statements":[{"q":"A claim about an illegal point must be made immediately, not later.","a":"H","why":"H 段：禁手必须在下一手之前提出，过后不受理。"},{"q":"A full round robin becomes too slow once the field grows.","a":"B","why":"B 段：人数一多，循环赛耗时太长，所以大场面用积分编排。"},{"q":"Each assistant referee is given a fixed group of boards.","a":"F","why":"F 段：辅助裁判分台负责，选手知道该找谁。"},{"q":"Tie-breaks are worked out and published together with the final results.","a":"J","why":"J 段：最后一局录入后计算破同分、公布名次与奖项。"},{"q":"Entries close early so that the pairing can be prepared in time.","a":"A","why":"A 段：报名提前一周截止，否则首轮编排会变成抽签。"},{"q":"A player may lose on time even with a better position.","a":"G","why":"G 段：包干制时间用尽即负，与局面无关。"},{"q":"Too few rounds may leave two players on the same score.","a":"C","why":"C 段：轮次不足会出现同分，冠军只能靠破同分决定。"},{"q":"A candidate must pass a written test and work at real events before being licensed.","a":"I","why":"I 段：三级裁判要笔试，还要在真实比赛中实习后才发证。"},{"q":"Referees have to check the fifth move before play goes on.","a":"D","why":"D 段：五手两打，裁判必须在继续落子前核对第五手。"},{"q":"A referee can see a whole court from one place instead of crossing the room.","a":"E","why":"E 段：分区之后噪音下降、走动变少，一名裁判能看住整个赛区。"}]},{"id":"m03","type":"matching","topic":"杂论","source":"洛阳五子棋月赛系列、全民健身月、迎新杯、果立方杯","title":"Ten Months, One Association","sections":[{"l":"A","t":"The calendar","text":"In 2026 the Luoyang Gomoku Association stopped waiting for a single big event and spread its season across the year. A monthly or venue-based tournament was held in February, March, April, May, September and October, each in a different place: an art space, a community centre, a department store. The first two stops were only a fortnight apart, because the New Year holiday and the start of the school term left no better window. Players began to treat the series as a calendar rather than as news."},{"l":"B","t":"The first stop","text":"The season opened on 22 February, the sixth day of the New Year holiday, in the Hui Fu Tang art space, which also serves as the city's training base. Two groups played side by side, an open group and an unrated group, over five rounds. Li Dan won the open group; the unrated group was won by Lü Zihao, who was ten years old. Grandfathers and schoolchildren sat at neighbouring boards, which the organisers described as the point of the day rather than an accident."},{"l":"C","t":"The community room","text":"The second stop moved into a residential community, in the party-mass service centre of the Tianyuan neighbourhood in Luolong District. It was organised by the association with support from its president unit, a local culture company, and it filled the room with players from the surrounding streets. Zhao Yan won the open group. Holding an event inside a community, rather than asking residents to travel to a gymnasium, brought in people who had never entered a tournament before."},{"l":"D","t":"The shopping mall","text":"The April stop was timed for the 43rd Peony Culture Festival and placed in the West Plaza Wangfujing department store, with boards laid out in the atrium. Three competitions ran at once: an open individual event, an unrated group for children, and a pairs event. Shoppers stopped to watch, and some of them asked how to enter the next one. A mall is noisy and has no proper playing hall, but it puts the game in front of people who would never look for it."},{"l":"E","t":"Who takes part","text":"The entry list of the April event shows how wide the field had become. University students travelled in from other districts; a strong international chess player came to test a different board game; a Go teacher brought a group of students to compare notes. The association's president unit covered the costs, and local companies provided prizes. None of these players belonged to a renju club, and most of them had learned the rules within the past two years."},{"l":"F","t":"The parent-child event","text":"On 23 May, ahead of Children's Day, the association held a family tournament with three groups: parent and child pairs, an unrated children's group, and ordinary pairs. Each game allowed twenty minutes, after which a clock was started with five extra minutes for each player. The youngest entrant was six years old and was playing his second tournament. Organisers said the children were quieter at the board than their parents, who tended to point at the stones."},{"l":"G","t":"Playing in pairs","text":"The pairs events are the association's own invention, and they are the loudest part of the day. Two players sit on the same side of the board, discuss every move in a low voice, and are not allowed to touch a stone until they agree. Wei Chenyi and Wei Ziqing won the parent-child group; Tang Bi and Pan Guanchen won the open pairs. Beginners like the format because a mistake is shared, and strong players like it because explaining a plan out loud is harder than playing it."},{"l":"H","t":"The September meeting room","text":"In September the series came back to the Tianyuan community, this time to a second-floor meeting room. There was no stage, no banner wall and no spectator area: office desks were pushed together in pairs to make playing tables, and the room fell silent as soon as the first round began. Players who wanted to think stood up and walked to the window. The organisers call this the plainest event of the year, and also the easiest to repeat, because it needs nothing that a community centre does not already have."},{"l":"I","t":"The autumn stop","text":"The last stop of the season returned to the same department store, on the fourth floor this time, at the end of October. Again there were three events, and again the entry list mixed children, students and older players. Holding two stops in one building was deliberate: the store's managers had seen the crowd in April, and the second visit cost the association almost nothing to arrange. The series ended where the year's biggest audience already was."},{"l":"J","t":"Why a monthly series","text":"The association's stated aim is a change of verb: to move players from \"make me play\" to \"I want to play\". A single annual championship produces one day of attention and eleven months of silence, while a monthly series gives a beginner a date to prepare for and a reason to come back. Entries still arrive on the morning of an event, which the organisers accept as a sign of interest rather than a problem. What the series builds is not a champion but a habit."}],"statements":[{"q":"Two players shared one side of the board and had to agree before touching a stone.","a":"G","why":"G 段讲双人联棋：两人同坐一侧、低声商议，未达成一致不能落子。"},{"q":"The season began during the New Year holiday.","a":"B","why":"B 段：2 月 22 日，丙午年大年初六，在晖福堂艺术交流空间开赛。"},{"q":"The organisers want players to ask for tournaments instead of being persuaded to attend.","a":"J","why":"J 段：协会的目标是把「要我下棋」变成「我要下棋」。"},{"q":"One event was arranged to coincide with a flower festival.","a":"D","why":"D 段：4 月那站赶在第 43 届洛阳牡丹文化节期间。"},{"q":"The first two stops of the year were held only two weeks apart.","a":"A","why":"A 段：前两站只隔两周，因为年假与开学之间没有更好的窗口。"},{"q":"The youngest player at one event was six years old.","a":"F","why":"F 段：六一亲子赛最小的选手六岁，已是第二次参赛。"},{"q":"An event took place in a neighbourhood party-mass service centre in March.","a":"C","why":"C 段：第二站走进洛龙区天元社区党群活动中心。"},{"q":"The same department store hosted the series twice in one year.","a":"I","why":"I 段：10 月底回到同一家百货，这次在四楼。"},{"q":"A player of another board game came to try his hand.","a":"E","why":"E 段：有国际象棋高手跨界挑战、围棋教师带队前来。"},{"q":"Office desks were pushed together to make playing tables.","a":"H","why":"H 段：9 月那站把办公桌两两拼成棋桌，没有观众席。"}],"gloss":[["fortnight","两星期"],["atrium","（商场）中庭"],["party-mass service centre","党群活动中心"],["unrated group","无级组"],["entrant","参赛者"],["entry list","报名表"]]}],"source":"renju.net / RIF International Rules of Renju"};
